Package: psf-security-patches
Version: @FULL@
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Architecture: all
Maintainer: @MAINT@
Installed-Size: @SIZE@
Depends: bash (>= 4.0)
Recommends: python3 (>= 3.8)
Replaces: psf-security-packages (<< 26.10.30)
Conflicts: psf-security-packages (<< 26.10.30)
Suggests: apparmor-utils, auditd
Homepage: https://privacysafe.app/docs/security-patches/
Description: PrivacySafe security checks, hunts and protection for serious vulnerabilities
 Free public domain tools for GNU/Linux servers. For each serious
 vulnerability they tell you whether you are exposed, look for signs that
 someone has already used the flaw, and, where there is a safe way to do it,
 protect the machine until you can update. Covers the October and
 November 2026 vulnerabilities in vsftpd, BTCPay Server, the Claude Code
 extension, Brotli, HAProxy,
 rsyslog, YOOtheme, Django REST framework, Expat, baserCMS, Ruby and
 GitLab, PHP2Shell, WordPress, nginx, Tomcat, the Linux kernel and more.
 .
 Start with: man psf-security-patches. Every tool has its own manual page
 (psf-NAME) and the whole set is in the info manual: info psf-security-patches.
 .
 On Red Hat family hosts, psf-rhsa (man psf-rhsa) finds Red Hat's security
 advisories for a CVE and checks the installed packages against Red Hat's
 fixes. It needs python3, which is recommended, not required.
 .
 The tools install under /opt/psf-security-patches, plus the php2shell-watch
 systemd unit, which is not enabled automatically. Updating is the permanent
 fix; the tools protect you until then and show whether you were hit.
 .
 Built and published by PrivacySafe Foundation, a 501(c)(3) nonprofit, with
